--TEST--
An expression used as its own statement is evaluated for its effects and its value discarded, whatever shape it is
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:statements/expression-statements-blocks-and-declarations
// PHP evaluates an expression statement for what it does and throws the value
// away. So does Novis: there is no "expression result unused" refusal, because
// "has no effect" is not a question the compiler can answer — a call is buried
// inside half of the shapes below, and a property read runs the hook `rule:classes/property-hooks`
// gives it. The agreement this pins is that every shape runs its call
// exactly once and in source order, which a slice that lowered only some of
// them would fail on while each line still looked plausible on its own.
class Note {
    public static function say(string $s): string {
        echo $s, "\n";
        return $s;
    }
}

int $i = 0;
string $tag = "t";

// A call buried in a discarded expression still runs, once.
Note::say("concat") . "x";
true ? Note::say("ternary") : Note::say("never");
match (1) { 1 => Note::say("match"), default => Note::say("never") };
[Note::say("array")];
// `rule:types/anonymous-object`: a statement-initial `{` is a block, so the literal is
// parenthesized — which changes nothing about what the statement means.
({v: Note::say("shape")});
(Note::say("paren"));

// The other half: legal, lowered, and observably nothing. A bare local is the
// one that must not be *released* here — it hands back the local's own value,
// and this frame never took the reference it would be dropping.
$i;
$tag;
1 + 2;
"literal";
$i < 1;

// The locals are untouched by having been mentioned.
echo $i, $tag, "\n";
--EXPECT--
concat
ternary
match
array
shape
paren
0t
